<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm of 5.0 cm by duplex ultrasound in a 70-year-old man, found at an exploratory laparotomy for abdominal pain and wrapped rather than repaired. Please advise on surveillance, the thresholds and options for elective repair, and medical therapy, and record a shared decision with him. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks, on the same day as his CT angiography results visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does the CT angiography confirm an aneurysm below the repair threshold, and does the old wrapping change his risk or the plan?</item><item>How often, and by which test, should the aneurysm be measured?</item><item>At what diameter, growth rate, or symptoms would you advise elective repair, and is his anatomy suitable for endovascular repair?</item><item>What medical therapy do you advise: blood pressure target, statin, and antiplatelet therapy?</item><item>Should other arteries be checked for aneurysms?</item><item>Please record a shared decision with him about repair, including his preferences.</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Einstein, a 70-year-old theoretical physicist, is seen at the request of Dr. Ward for an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm. Episodes of severe abdominal pain led to an exploratory laparotomy, at which the aneurysm was found and its front wrapped rather than repaired; the pain has not returned. He has no back, flank, or groin pain, fainting, claudication, or cold or painful toes. He stopped his pipe three weeks ago and uses a nicotine patch and lozenges. He takes amlodipine, losartan, which was started four weeks ago, and atorvastatin. He walks an hour a day and has no chest pain or breathlessness on exertion. He asked for exact figures and for the reasoning behind each recommendation. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Albert Einstein was born on March 14, 1879, in Ulm, in the Kingdom of Wurttemberg, German Empire. [D]</item><item>He worked at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton from 1933 until his death and became a United States citizen in 1940. [D]</item><item>At about 69 he began to have intermittent episodes of severe abdominal pain, at times bad enough to make him vomit, which grew worse toward the end of 1948. [D]</item><item>On December 31, 1948, at the Jewish Hospital of Brooklyn, the surgeon Rudolph Nissen performed an exploratory laparotomy and found an abdominal aortic aneurysm described as the size of a grapefruit; because it could not be removed with the surgery of the time, he wrapped its front in cellophane to provoke scarring that might prevent growth and rupture. [D]</item><item>He recovered slowly and left the hospital about three weeks after the operation. [D]</item><item>Einstein was a long-time pipe smoker; a pipe was characteristic of his appearance, and an account of his aneurysm says he had smoked constantly since adolescence. [D]</item><item>Retrospective writers have suggested that he had features of the autism spectrum. [R]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

